**Female pharaoh of Egypt's 18th Dynasty (c. 1507–1458 BCE).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Her reign emphasized trade and monumental building, including her temple at Deir el-Bahri.

**Chinese emperor (156–87 BCE) who greatly expanded Han power and encouraged routes later called the Silk Road.**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- He made Confucian learning central to government.

**China's only woman to rule as emperor in her own name (624–705).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- She expanded the civil service examinations and governed during a period of Tang-era strength.

**Byzantine empress (c. 497–548) and influential partner of Justinian I.**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- She backed legal protections for women and helped steady the government during the Nika riots.

**Founder of the Mongol Empire (c. 1162–1227).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- He united Mongol tribes and built a disciplined conquest state that became the basis of history's largest contiguous land empire.

**Ruler of the Mali Empire (c. 1280–c. 1337).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Renowned for wealth, a spectacular pilgrimage to Mecca, and patronage that strengthened Timbuktu as a centre of learning.

**Ottoman sultan (1432–1481).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Known as “the Conqueror.” He captured Constantinople in 1453, ending the Byzantine Empire and making the city an Ottoman capital.

**Ottoman sultan (1494–1566) whose empire reached a political and cultural high point.**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- He expanded its territory and codified laws, earning the Turkish title “the Lawgiver.”

**Japanese warlord (1543–1616) who won at Sekigahara and founded the Tokugawa shogunate.**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Its rule brought more than two centuries of internal peace.

**British monarch (1819–1901) whose 63-year reign gave its name to the Victorian era.**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Britain industrialized and its empire expanded enormously during her rule.

**Zulu king (c. 1787–1828) who built a powerful state in southern Africa through military and political reorganization.**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Accounts of him mix evidence with later legend.

**Emperor of Ethiopia (1844–1913).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- He expanded and modernized the state, and Ethiopian forces defeated an Italian invasion at Adwa in 1896.

**Polish astronomer (1473–1543).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Proposed a mathematical model placing the Sun, rather than Earth, near the centre of the planetary system.

**Arab scholar (c. 965–c. 1040), also called Alhazen.**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- His experiments on light and vision established that sight occurs when light enters the eye.

**Persian physician and philosopher (c. 980–1037).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- His Canon of Medicine organized medical knowledge and remained influential for centuries across Eurasia.

**Persian mathematician (c. 780–c. 850) whose work systematized algebra.**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- The words “algorithm” and, through his book title, “algebra” are linked to him.

**Han Chinese polymath (78–139).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- He advanced astronomy and mathematics and devised an early seismoscope that indicated the direction of distant earthquakes.

**Serbian-American inventor (1856–1943) central to practical alternating-current power systems.**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- His work included induction motors, transformers, and high-frequency electrical experiments.

**British mathematician (1815–1852).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Her notes on Babbage's Analytical Engine included an algorithm, often described as the first published computer program.

**Chinese-American experimental physicist (1912–1997).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Her 1956 experiment showed that parity symmetry is violated in weak nuclear interactions.

**American mathematician (1918–2020).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Orbital calculations supported early NASA crewed missions, including John Glenn's flight and Apollo lunar missions.

**Chinese pharmaceutical chemist (born 1930).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Drew on traditional texts to help discover artemisinin, a highly effective treatment for malaria.

**Japanese court writer (c. 973–c. 1014 or 1025).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Author of The Tale of Genji, often called the world's first novel.

**English novelist (1775–1817).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Pride and Prejudice and her other novels use wit and social observation to examine class, money, and marriage.

**Russian writer (1828–1910), author of War and Peace and Anna Karenina.**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- His later Christian pacifism influenced nonviolent reformers.

**Dutch Post-Impressionist painter (1853–1890).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- His vivid colour and expressive brushwork shaped modern art; The Starry Night is among his best-known works.

**Mexican painter (1907–1954).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Known for psychologically intense self-portraits exploring identity, pain, disability, and Mexican culture.

**Nigerian novelist (1930–2013).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Things Fall Apart portrayed Igbo society and colonial disruption, reshaping global understanding of modern African literature.

**Traditionally regarded as an ancient Chinese strategist associated with The Art of War.**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- His exact identity and the text's authorship are debated by historians.

**Traditionally credited as the ancient Chinese author of the Dao De Jing and a foundational figure in Daoism.**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Historians debate whether he was one person.

**Alexandrian philosopher, mathematician, and astronomer (c. 355–415).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- A prominent teacher in late antiquity, her murder became a symbol of sectarian conflict.

**English writer (1759–1797).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- A Vindication of the Rights of Woman argued that women deserve education and rational equality.

**Chinese Muslim admiral (1371–1433).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Led seven enormous Ming voyages across the Indian Ocean, reaching Southeast Asia, Arabia, and East Africa.

**Moroccan traveller (1304–1368/69).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Journeyed across Africa, Eurasia, and the Indian Ocean world, covering more distance than Marco Polo.

**Lemhi Shoshone woman (c. 1788–after 1812).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Interpreted and helped guide the Lewis and Clark expedition across western North America.

**American aviation pioneer (1897–disappeared 1937).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- She was the first woman to fly solo across the Atlantic and vanished during an attempted round-the-world flight.

**Soviet cosmonaut (1934–1968).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Became the first human in space, completing one orbit of Earth aboard Vostok 1 in 1961.

**Ra'iatean navigator and priest (c. 1725–1770).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- His Pacific knowledge, translation, and charting were vital during James Cook's first voyage.

**Formerly enslaved Haitian revolutionary leader (c. 1743–1803).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- He helped defeat slavery and foreign armies, laying foundations for Haitian independence.

**Venezuelan military and political leader (1783–1830).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Helped liberate several South American territories from Spanish rule, earning the title “El Libertador.”

**Argentine general (1778–1850) central to the independence of Argentina, Chile, and Peru.**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- His army famously crossed the Andes in 1817.

**Mexican revolutionary (1879–1919).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Demanded land reform under the slogan “Land and Liberty.” His movement represented rural communities in southern Mexico.

**American abolitionist, writer, and orator (c. 1818–1895).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- After escaping slavery, his autobiographies and speeches powerfully argued for emancipation and equal rights.

**British suffrage leader (1858–1928).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Founded the Women's Social and Political Union, known for militant campaigning for women's voting rights.

**Kenyan environmentalist and democracy activist (1940–2011).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- Founded the Green Belt Movement and became the first African woman awarded the Nobel Peace Prize.

## Cleopatra VII

{#0F766E}**Why this fits**{/}

The last active Ptolemaic ruler used both alliances to defend Egypt's independence as Roman power expanded.

## Qin Shi Huang

{#0F766E}**Why this fits**{/}

He became China's first emperor in 221 BCE and imposed empire-wide standards.

## Zheng He

{#0F766E}**Why this fits**{/}

His early-15th-century fleets visited ports from Southeast Asia to East Africa.

## Toussaint Louverture

{#0F766E}**Why this fits**{/}

His leadership helped destroy slavery in Saint-Domingue and prepare the way for independent Haiti.

## Ada Lovelace

{#0F766E}**Why this fits**{/}

Her 1843 notes described steps for calculating Bernoulli numbers and anticipated general-purpose computing.

## Rabindranath Tagore

{#0F766E}**Why this fits**{/}

The Bengali poet received the 1913 prize; his writing and songs influenced culture across South Asia.

**New Zealand suffrage leader (1848–1934).**

{#0F766E}Why they matter{/}

- She organized petitions and campaigning that helped New Zealand become the first self-governing country where women won national voting rights in 1893.
